  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4b/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_94.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A rocket carrying a nuclear warhead suddenly veers off course, forcing the US \"bomb-testing site\" to attempt a mid-air detonation to prevent it from striking at some random location on Earth.&nbsp; But they find something preventing their \"destruct\" signal from getting through.&nbsp; Thinking that there's only one person on Earth who could possibly get to the runaway missile in time, a worldwide cry for Thor is signaled to contact the Pentagon at once!&nbsp; Don Blake hears the urgent message over the radio from his medical office, and after a quick change, calls in to assure the authorities that he's on his way.\n\nThe scene then shifts to Asgard, where we see Loki, bound to a cliff by uru manacles.&nbsp; He is, of course, responsible for the missile's malfunction, hoping that the crisis would lure Thor out into the open.&nbsp; And even as he watches Thor hurl his hammer at the missile to ensure its destruction, he uses all his remaining \"magical energy\" to distract Thor at the precise moment his hammer returns to his hand.\n\nAs he had hoped, the distraction works such that the returning hammer strikes Thor in the head instead of being caught <i>by</i> him -- right in the \"chromosomatic gland, which determines and changes personality!\"&nbsp; In seconds, Thor's entire nature is transformed into evil.\n\nSuddenly responding to a thought planted in his head by Loki to come to Asgard and free him, Thor arrives soon afterwards and attacks Heimdall on Bifrost, calling him a \"flunky.\"&nbsp; Thor then sees Loki bound with uru bonds, and forgetting he's an arch-enemy, frees him.&nbsp; Odin and his men confront them, and Loki realizes that the two of them can't defeat all of Asgard united against them, so he instead convinces Thor to take him to Earth where they can cause havoc, thus turning Earth into Loki's cosmic playground, holding it hostage until Odin agrees to \"yield control of Asgard to Thor and me!\"\n\nOnce on Earth, Loki convinces Thor to summon thunder, lightning and so forth \"to destroy man's proudest achievements.\"&nbsp; Soon the Taj Mahal, Eiffel Tower, Golden Gate Bridge, Panama Canal, and the Leaning Tower of Pisa are destroyed by Thor while Loki uses his own magic abilities to animate the Sphinx, rock the Empire State Building and so forth -- it's an absolute rampage on humanity!\n\nFinally, various representatives of the United Nations approach the brothers and beg them to contact Odin to ask that they be allowed to take over Asgard so they'll leave Earth alone.&nbsp; But when Thor contacts Asgard, the hammer suddenly suspends in air and then drops on Thor's head, in the same spot as before.&nbsp; To Loki's horror, the representatives turn out to actually be Odin and the gods of Asgard, <i>not</i> the various members of the U.N. after all!\n\nThor is back to normal and swears to the people of Earth that he will repair all the damage.&nbsp; And Odin then adds that the \"memory of these events shall be erased from the minds of men!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "Loki schemes, landmarks fall, morality collapses, and the plot evaporates.\nA comic so empty it feels like the universe itself hit CTRL‑Z.",
  "subtitle": "When Blunt-Force Trauma Overpowers the Will to Live",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | LongHandle | width=20%] Before I go any farther, can the handle of Thor's hammer <i>possibly</i> get any longer than it is shown on the splash page?&nbsp; I mean, <i>wow</i> -- that <b>has</b> to be 3 feet long and easily half of Thor's height!&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Contrast that to the later drawings of John Byrne, who I thought always drew the handle way too short!]\n[BR]\n[BR] It's less a hammer and more a medieval polearm at this point, and honestly?\n[BR] It's almost impossible to take the rest of the book seriously once you notice it.&nbsp; <b><i>Whoa</i></b><i>!</i>\n[BR]\n[BR] And incidentally, I'm <i>loving</i> Loki's evil grin on that splash page as well!\n\nAs for the story itself, there's just no escaping the fact that it was <i>dreadful</i> -- just [SPAN: severity=error; text=absolutely poor writing] -- a completely made-up \"chromosomatic gland\" responsible for personality, whereupon being struck in the head where it is apparently located, it causes one to completely reverse his sense of right and wrong?\n[ASSET: center | ItWorked]\n\nThe story treats this revelation with all the scientific rigor of a fortune cookie.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Ugh</i>!&nbsp; Please give me a break!]\n\n[ASSET: left | HoldEarthHostage | width=12%] One misguided plot beat follows another until Thor has freed the bound Loki in Asgard and the two brothers in evil travel to Earth to wreak havoc and try to force Odin into turning over control of Asgard to Loki, leaving the All-Father to wonder aloud what could have caused such an about-face in his son's nature.\n\nThor's heel‑turn is so instantaneous it feels like someone flipped a light switch labeled: [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=\"EVIL\"]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhat follows over the next three pages is Thor and Loki's attack on all of Earth's most famous landmarks.\n[ASSET: center | DestructionOnEarth | width=67%]\n\nThis is nothing less than global carnage presented with all the emotional weight of a sightseeing montage [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(just with more ominous background music)].\n\nTerror mounts in \"every quarter of the globe\" until representatives of the United Nations finally approach the brothers and beg them to contact Odin and surrender control of Asgard.\n\nLoki agrees, believing that Odin won't be able to resist humanity's own appeal to him to save Earth, and of course, by the last page, we learn that the U.N. representatives are actually Odin and some other Asgardians who are disguised in order to draw Thor close enough to them to allow them to recreate the blow to the head by the hammer to restore Thor to normal.\n\nIt's a plan so convoluted you'd think Loki wrote it, not Odin... or even Stan...\n\nIn a gag-inducing final sequence, Thor promises the people of Earth that the gods of Asgard will \"repair all the damage done to your planet!&nbsp; Everything shall become once again as it was!\"\n[ASSET: center | ResetButtonPushed | width=67%]\n\nAfterward, Loki is re-chained to the wall, and Odin assures Thor that, though evil may always exist, \"there will always be a <i>champion</i> to challenge and defeat it!&nbsp; A champion such as <i>Mighty Thor</i>...noblest of the gods, protector of mankind!\"\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThere can only be one possible response to this pathetic excuse of a story:\n\n[SPAN: display=block; textalign=center; severity=error; fontsize=extralarge; fonttype=bold-italic; text= ****** BARF! ******]\n\nLet's see now:\n[HANG] * a completely made-up gland that controls personality\n[HANG] * more remote-control manipulation of Thor from a bound-up Loki\n[HANG] * destruction of thousands of years of human landmarks\n[HANG] * all restored by the Asgardian gods by page 13, as if nothing at all happened\n[HANG] * memories of all the devastation on Earth \"erased from the minds of men\"\n[HANG] * a crisis so massive it should reshape global history, treated as a minor inconvenience\n\nOh, and let's not forget:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=RESET BUTTON PUSHED]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=<i>Yep</i>, you guessed it -- this book had it all!]\n\nIf stories like this are what one is to expect from the \"Marvel Age of Comics\" that the cover promises, please count me out!&nbsp; Issues like this make the slogan feel less like a promise and more like a threat.\n\nIt really does seem like Stan has books that he favors in these early days of Marvel and focuses heavily on them (i.e. <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> and to a lesser extent <i>Fantastic Four</i>), but then just throws out inane plots for some of the other titles and lets his scriptwriters run with them.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Sometimes you wonder if there's a plot dart board in his office...)]\n\nOne has to wonder if he himself cringes whenever he reads the final results -- like the dreck here that extracted yet <i>another</i> 12 cents from readers, who have to wonder... [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Why am I <b>doing</b> this?]\n\nAnd once again -- the handle on that hammer -- [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=italic; text=Holy cow!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Let's just put it this way:&nbsp; Thor won't need a <b>cane</b> when he gets old!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | DrainedOfAllStrength | width=15%] The comical length of the handle and Sinnott's wonderfully evil grin on Loki's face on the splash page were the only moments of entertainment value in a story that otherwise had me questioning my life choices.\n[BR]\n[BR] My recommendation to potential readers out there?\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=DON'T!]\n[BR]\n[BR] If you do, you will become as Loki was on page 4:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text= ...drained of all strength, unable to remain conscious.]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=This issue doesn't just miss the mark -- it forgets what the mark was.]",
  "side_note": [
    "The second panel on page 4 indicates that Loki is shackled to a cliff with uru \"menacles,\" but that is obviously a misspelling.&nbsp; I believe the correct word is \"manacles.\"&nbsp; The mistake is made again at the bottom of page 6."
  ],
  "highlight": "Thor gets bonked on the head, destroys the world with Loki, and everything's magically fine again by the end of page 13."
}